1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,740
Okay, welcome back to another one of our videos.

2
00:00:04,740 --> 00:00:08,920
This one is all about the risks of oxygen use in the EMS setting.

3
00:00:08,920 --> 00:00:12,280
Specifically, we want to try and answer a few pointed questions.

4
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:15,140
So first is when should we utilize oxygen?

5
00:00:15,140 --> 00:00:18,260
And then following on from that are two other questions.

6
00:00:18,260 --> 00:00:20,800
When do we need to use 100% oxygen?

7
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:26,000
And then is there ever a time where we need to give oxygen up to a goal of an SPO2 of

8
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:27,000
100%?

9
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:32,600
And then the other general question here is when do we need to withhold oxygen?

10
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:35,000
Basically we're going to try and answer this question.

11
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Is oxygen the bad guy?

12
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:42,120
There's a lot of discussion these days about the negative consequences of giving oxygen

13
00:00:42,120 --> 00:00:43,840
to all different kinds of patients.

14
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:48,880
So we just want to look into that and make sure these aren't false accusations against

15
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:51,160
our dear friend oxygen.

16
00:00:51,160 --> 00:00:55,880
This next slide just shows the specific adverse effects of oxygen that we're going to talk

17
00:00:55,880 --> 00:00:57,880
about in this presentation.

18
00:00:57,880 --> 00:01:00,560
But just know that that is not an exhaustive list.

19
00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:04,720
There are lots of other things that could be discussed but we focus on the ones that

20
00:01:04,720 --> 00:01:09,880
are most relevant and most often brought up in the EMS setting.

21
00:01:09,880 --> 00:01:15,640
Okay and then just for a little bit of context, this is why we decided to pick this video.

22
00:01:15,640 --> 00:01:20,000
We won't read it all to you but it's all spelled out there for you.

23
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,760
And then just before we get into everything, just know that you're probably not going to

24
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:28,240
come away from this video, from this presentation with life changing knowledge.

25
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:32,480
Like everyone at this point pretty much knows that we give oxygen in moderation and that

26
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:36,040
there are risks associated with giving oxygen in excess.

27
00:01:36,040 --> 00:01:39,520
This is more of a deep dive into the specifics of why that is.

28
00:01:39,520 --> 00:01:46,160
Okay, so the first thing to talk about is oxygen toxicity and free radicals.

29
00:01:46,160 --> 00:01:52,720
So oxygen toxicity is kind of a generic term that we use for damage caused by oxygen.

30
00:01:52,720 --> 00:01:56,880
That said, it's not the O2 molecule itself that is generally the one causing damage.

31
00:01:56,880 --> 00:02:00,360
It's these other forms of oxygen that you can see listed here.

32
00:02:00,360 --> 00:02:05,840
And then of these other species which we can collectively call reactive oxygen species,

33
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:08,560
some of them are what we call free radicals.

34
00:02:08,560 --> 00:02:14,080
So molecules with a free electron in the outer shell, we're calling back to chemistry class

35
00:02:14,080 --> 00:02:15,080
in high school.

36
00:02:15,080 --> 00:02:19,400
So just to summarize these different terms, oxygen toxicity is damage caused by oxygen

37
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:23,160
but it's actually caused by these other reactive oxygen species.

38
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:28,560
And then some of these species such as the superoxide or the hydroxyl radical, those

39
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:33,160
are free radicals even though we kind of lump them all together sometimes.

40
00:02:33,160 --> 00:02:38,960
And then this next slide is just to point out where these species come from.

41
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:44,360
So it's not like oxygen is just floating around as an O2 molecule in your blood and it spontaneously

42
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:46,760
splits into these other forms.

43
00:02:46,760 --> 00:02:49,600
These other forms come up because there are different reactions in your body because you're

44
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:54,120
exposed to different things in your environments because you have different like pathologic

45
00:02:54,120 --> 00:02:55,240
processes.

46
00:02:55,240 --> 00:02:58,900
So no need to get into the details here but this is just pointing out that there are lots

47
00:02:58,900 --> 00:03:03,280
of different sources of these reactive oxygen species.

48
00:03:03,280 --> 00:03:07,240
And just for a little bit of context, there was some data from years ago that shows that

49
00:03:07,240 --> 00:03:15,160
giving oxygen in high concentration to neonates in a NICU setting resulted in visual impairments.

50
00:03:15,160 --> 00:03:19,480
So this connection of like oxygen causes babies to go blind is why there's a lot of

51
00:03:19,480 --> 00:03:25,160
pushback against giving high concentration oxygen in the healthcare setting in general.

52
00:03:25,160 --> 00:03:30,880
But just recognize that reactive oxygen species are a normal physiologic thing.

53
00:03:30,880 --> 00:03:36,280
They have some benefit in the body and they are important component of a lot of different

54
00:03:36,280 --> 00:03:37,280
reactions.

55
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:38,280
So they're not all bad.

56
00:03:38,920 --> 00:03:45,200
It's also worth keeping in mind that not all of these reactive species come from giving

57
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:46,960
oxygen by itself.

58
00:03:46,960 --> 00:03:50,720
We showed a couple sides back there are lots of different sources of these reactive oxygen

59
00:03:50,720 --> 00:03:55,220
species other than high concentration oxygen.

60
00:03:55,220 --> 00:04:01,300
And there's some data that shows that actually giving oxygen can help clear those reactive

61
00:04:01,300 --> 00:04:07,800
oxygen species out of the system when they've been created by some other mechanism.

62
00:04:07,800 --> 00:04:12,400
To summarize it here, yes, damage from too much oxygen is a thing.

63
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:15,680
And again, we'll talk about some of the specifics here shortly.

64
00:04:15,680 --> 00:04:19,280
But also reactive oxygen species are necessary.

65
00:04:19,280 --> 00:04:23,480
Some oxygen may actually help clear these reactive oxygen species a little bit faster

66
00:04:23,480 --> 00:04:25,840
than if you didn't have any oxygen at all.

67
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:29,520
So basically just use oxygen in moderation.

68
00:04:29,520 --> 00:04:30,520
Cool.

69
00:04:30,520 --> 00:04:35,400
So the next thing to talk about is alterations in respiratory drive.

70
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:38,040
And again, this is related to the use of oxygen.

71
00:04:38,040 --> 00:04:40,140
So on the left here is normal.

72
00:04:40,140 --> 00:04:45,280
So the normal stimulus for either breathing or taking more breaths or increasing your

73
00:04:45,280 --> 00:04:49,240
respiratory rate would be an increase in CO2 or carbon dioxide.

74
00:04:49,240 --> 00:04:53,800
Now, if that mechanism fails, you also have a mechanism by which you are triggered to

75
00:04:53,800 --> 00:04:58,720
breathe if you become hypoxic or if you're in a state of acidosis or a stress response,

76
00:04:58,720 --> 00:04:59,720
all of these things.

77
00:04:59,720 --> 00:05:03,660
But normally it follows like in a kind of stepwise process based on like your body's

78
00:05:03,660 --> 00:05:05,580
perceived hierarchy of needs.

79
00:05:05,580 --> 00:05:10,260
The idea with a hypoxic drive, and again, this is an idea, this is not true, but the

80
00:05:10,260 --> 00:05:15,520
idea here was that if you have a patient with a chronic respiratory condition who lives

81
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:19,980
at baseline with an elevated CO2, that mechanism is not working.

82
00:05:19,980 --> 00:05:24,700
So cross that CO2 stimulus off the list because it doesn't work very well.

83
00:05:24,700 --> 00:05:29,520
So then their primary response to breathe faster or to take a breath or to increase

84
00:05:29,520 --> 00:05:32,220
the respiratory rate is hypoxia.

85
00:05:32,220 --> 00:05:36,460
So the thought, and again, this is not actually how it works, but this is the thought is that

86
00:05:36,460 --> 00:05:40,420
if that were true and then you give these patients oxygen, you take away their respiratory

87
00:05:40,420 --> 00:05:41,420
drive.

88
00:05:41,420 --> 00:05:46,980
So the thought is that giving a chronic lung patient supplemental oxygen would cause them

89
00:05:46,980 --> 00:05:47,980
to stop breathing.

90
00:05:47,980 --> 00:05:52,060
It would take away the respiratory drive and then they would obviously have a bad outcome

91
00:05:52,060 --> 00:05:53,500
because they're not breathing.

92
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:55,340
In reality, that's not exactly how it works.

93
00:05:55,340 --> 00:05:59,920
There are two other effects at play here and we're going to talk about each one of them

94
00:05:59,920 --> 00:06:00,920
in turn.

95
00:06:00,920 --> 00:06:06,560
So the first one is VQ mismatch and I put in parentheses under their hypoxic pulmonary

96
00:06:06,560 --> 00:06:07,960
vasoconstriction.

97
00:06:07,960 --> 00:06:09,760
So let's just show how that works.

98
00:06:09,760 --> 00:06:14,300
Okay, so real quick here, just for some review, we have two sketches.

99
00:06:14,300 --> 00:06:18,000
Each has an avioli and a corresponding blood supply.

100
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:20,320
These are both examples of VQ mismatch.

101
00:06:20,320 --> 00:06:21,840
They're just two different flavors.

102
00:06:21,840 --> 00:06:24,320
So on the left is what we call dead space.

103
00:06:24,320 --> 00:06:28,480
So this one we have an avioli that's fully inflated, but not enough blood flow.

104
00:06:28,480 --> 00:06:34,000
So there is air that's getting to that avioli, but not enough corresponding blood supply.

105
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:36,960
On the right hand side, we have what we call a shunt.

106
00:06:36,960 --> 00:06:41,320
So we have an underinflated avioli and an excess of blood.

107
00:06:41,320 --> 00:06:46,340
Now what the body normally does is in the right hand situation, it knows it's not getting

108
00:06:46,340 --> 00:06:47,420
enough air.

109
00:06:47,420 --> 00:06:52,580
So it vasoconstricts that vessel so that perfusion matches ventilation.

110
00:06:52,580 --> 00:06:57,060
So we call that response hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction.

111
00:06:57,060 --> 00:07:01,700
So just to show you in a video that we put from someone else, if you see on the left

112
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:07,680
hand side here, V equals Q, like there's a match between air flow and blood flow.

113
00:07:07,680 --> 00:07:12,940
On the right hand side in the chronic lung patient, you're not getting enough air flow.

114
00:07:12,940 --> 00:07:16,140
So the body compensates by vasoconstricting that vessel.

115
00:07:16,140 --> 00:07:19,260
So now all the V's match all the Q's and everything is fine.

116
00:07:19,260 --> 00:07:22,420
This is the normal response to the situation.

117
00:07:22,420 --> 00:07:24,600
So that's what the body did to fix the problem.

118
00:07:24,600 --> 00:07:29,000
Now if we were to give oxygen to this patient, we kind of mess with the system a little bit.

119
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:34,120
So again, we have normal on the left, but on the right we see the patient already compensated

120
00:07:34,120 --> 00:07:36,640
by vasoconstricting, but now we're giving oxygen.

121
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:39,240
So the body thinks it needs to vasodilate.

122
00:07:39,240 --> 00:07:43,960
The result of that is a reversal of that hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction.

123
00:07:43,960 --> 00:07:48,640
And what we get, if you see in the red there, you see an elevated CO2.

124
00:07:48,640 --> 00:07:54,480
So basically we're getting more CO2 dumped into the avioli with no way to actually exhale.

125
00:07:54,680 --> 00:08:01,680
So by giving oxygen to the chronic lung patient, we're increasing their PCO2, which is counter

126
00:08:01,800 --> 00:08:04,320
to what we generally want to be doing.

127
00:08:04,320 --> 00:08:08,520
The other way this happens is via what we call the Haldane effect.

128
00:08:08,520 --> 00:08:15,520
So basically it says that hemoglobin's affinity for CO2 is affected by the amount of oxygen.

129
00:08:16,360 --> 00:08:22,360
So basically in the chronic lung patient, they have an elevated amount of CO2 at baseline.

130
00:08:22,360 --> 00:08:25,880
So some of that CO2 ends up on the hemoglobin.

131
00:08:25,880 --> 00:08:31,360
Now when we give that patient supplemental oxygen, oxygen displaces all of that CO2 into

132
00:08:31,360 --> 00:08:32,600
the bloodstream.

133
00:08:32,600 --> 00:08:39,600
So our arterial concentration of CO2 increases by this other mechanism as well.

134
00:08:40,640 --> 00:08:43,080
So we have two mechanisms.

135
00:08:43,080 --> 00:08:45,960
The first is our VQ mismatch.

136
00:08:45,960 --> 00:08:51,920
When we reverse hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction by giving supplemental oxygen, the second

137
00:08:51,920 --> 00:08:56,720
is the Haldane effect where we displace a bunch of CO2 into the bloodstream by giving

138
00:08:56,720 --> 00:08:57,840
oxygen.

139
00:08:57,840 --> 00:09:03,040
So by both of these mechanisms or the combination of the two of them, we can end up in a state

140
00:09:03,040 --> 00:09:10,040
where our PACO2, so our arterial concentration of carbon dioxide, gets really high and that

141
00:09:12,160 --> 00:09:17,520
can in fact lead to decreased level of consciousness and respiratory depression.

142
00:09:18,520 --> 00:09:24,120
So just to summarize this here, we were talking about alterations in respiratory drive due

143
00:09:24,120 --> 00:09:25,760
to oxygen delivery.

144
00:09:25,760 --> 00:09:32,080
This is most frequently discussed in the context of patients with COPD or chronic lung conditions.

145
00:09:32,080 --> 00:09:38,840
So short answer is that no hypoxic drive is not really a thing, but yes, excessive CO2

146
00:09:38,840 --> 00:09:42,240
can cause alterations in respiratory drive.

147
00:09:42,240 --> 00:09:46,880
But that said, it's uncommon, it's a little bit nuanced, and it's not actually due to this

148
00:09:46,880 --> 00:09:50,560
hypoxic drive thing, it's due to other mechanisms.

149
00:09:50,560 --> 00:09:56,480
So moral of the story here, it's totally a-okay to give oxygen to a chronic lung patient.

150
00:09:56,480 --> 00:10:01,360
And then just as with all good things in life, moderation here is key.

151
00:10:01,360 --> 00:10:08,360
Okay, so next on our list of things to discuss is what we call absorption atelectasis.

152
00:10:08,520 --> 00:10:14,040
So here's the idea with this is that if we give someone 100% oxygen, what we do is we

153
00:10:14,040 --> 00:10:21,040
displace all of the nitrogen out of their avioli, so it's just filled with oxygen, right?

154
00:10:21,400 --> 00:10:27,040
Then if we have a portion of the lung or a specific avioli that doesn't have good airflow

155
00:10:27,040 --> 00:10:32,040
to it, let's say we have like mucus or some sort of obstruction, what happens is that

156
00:10:32,040 --> 00:10:37,160
all of the air from the avioli goes into the bloodstream and then the avioli collapses.

157
00:10:37,160 --> 00:10:40,600
You'll have a difference basically of the obstructed avioli collapse all the way, the

158
00:10:40,600 --> 00:10:42,280
other ones work just fine.

159
00:10:42,280 --> 00:10:45,820
So then you end up with atelectasis diffused across the lungs.

160
00:10:45,820 --> 00:10:47,640
So that's the theory.

161
00:10:47,640 --> 00:10:52,740
In reality, it may not be that relevant clinically.

162
00:10:52,740 --> 00:10:57,840
So there were some older studies that kind of looked at this idea and they sort of identified

163
00:10:57,840 --> 00:11:02,480
that it does happen to some degree, but probably not that big of a deal where it affects clinical

164
00:11:02,480 --> 00:11:03,480
outcomes.

165
00:11:03,480 --> 00:11:08,720
And then there's a few more recent studies that kind of found the same thing that yes,

166
00:11:08,720 --> 00:11:11,920
the effect does exist to a degree that can be measured.

167
00:11:11,920 --> 00:11:14,480
But that said, it's mostly in specific cases.

168
00:11:14,480 --> 00:11:18,920
So with no peeps, so we're not manually maintaining those avioli open.

169
00:11:18,920 --> 00:11:22,480
And then also with older patients who, you know, the assumption here is that they also

170
00:11:22,480 --> 00:11:24,360
have multiple comorbidities.

171
00:11:24,360 --> 00:11:25,440
Okay.

172
00:11:25,440 --> 00:11:28,080
So summarize absorption atelectasis.

173
00:11:28,080 --> 00:11:30,280
Yes, it's a thing.

174
00:11:30,280 --> 00:11:34,500
However, the effect is probably exaggerated and may not be clinically relevant.

175
00:11:34,500 --> 00:11:37,520
So totally fine to use oxygen again in moderation.

176
00:11:38,400 --> 00:11:38,900
All right.

177
00:11:38,900 --> 00:11:45,160
Next thing to talk about is impaired cardiac function with the use of supplemental oxygen.

178
00:11:45,160 --> 00:11:49,400
So nowadays we don't routinely give oxygen to patients who are just having chest pain

179
00:11:49,400 --> 00:11:53,860
or we suspect might be having an MI or even if they are having an MI, unless they are

180
00:11:53,860 --> 00:11:55,820
demonstrably hypoxic.

181
00:11:55,820 --> 00:11:58,520
The data on this goes back 50 years or so.

182
00:11:58,520 --> 00:12:04,480
So this paper was written in 1972 and it actually cites prior experiments that kind of showed

183
00:12:04,480 --> 00:12:05,600
the same thing.

184
00:12:05,600 --> 00:12:11,280
So they looked at different hemodynamic parameters using invasive cardiac monitoring and determined

185
00:12:11,280 --> 00:12:16,040
that some of those parameters decrease with use of supplemental oxygen.

186
00:12:16,040 --> 00:12:20,220
Since then, a lot of these experiments have been repeated multiple times.

187
00:12:20,220 --> 00:12:24,800
So this snapshot here is from like a narrative review that talks about a lot of different

188
00:12:24,800 --> 00:12:30,000
studies that have been done about the potential negative consequences of using supplemental

189
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:31,000
oxygen.

190
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:35,960
This is just a snapshot of their discussion about cardiac function specifically.

191
00:12:35,960 --> 00:12:38,000
And then two recent trials to mention.

192
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:43,300
This first one here was done in 2015 and it showed that supplemental oxygen can increase

193
00:12:43,300 --> 00:12:47,760
in FARC size when assessed at six months after an AMI.

194
00:12:47,760 --> 00:12:52,480
And then the second one showed that the use of supplemental oxygen in patients that aren't

195
00:12:52,480 --> 00:12:58,400
known to be hypoxic, so just routinely giving supplemental oxygen increases one year mortality

196
00:12:58,400 --> 00:13:00,520
after an AMI.

197
00:13:00,520 --> 00:13:04,760
And then one last thing to mention about both of these papers is that the authors, again,

198
00:13:04,760 --> 00:13:09,840
in both studies attribute the increase in mortality with the use of supplemental oxygen

199
00:13:09,840 --> 00:13:14,680
to the production of and the effects caused by reactive oxygen species, the same things

200
00:13:14,680 --> 00:13:17,200
that we talked about early in this presentation.

201
00:13:17,200 --> 00:13:21,040
Okay, so let's just summarize this section here.

202
00:13:21,040 --> 00:13:25,760
Yes, impaired cardiac function with supplemental oxygen is a thing.

203
00:13:25,760 --> 00:13:31,040
It occurs via that reactive oxygen species mechanism that we discussed previously.

204
00:13:31,040 --> 00:13:37,040
Yes, the effect can be clinically relevant, but also just keep in mind that profound hypoxia

205
00:13:37,040 --> 00:13:39,720
is not great for cardiac function either.

206
00:13:39,720 --> 00:13:42,720
So you guessed it, use oxygen in moderation.

207
00:13:42,720 --> 00:13:49,320
All right, next thing on our list to discuss is worse outcomes in neurologic injury with

208
00:13:49,320 --> 00:13:52,080
the use of supplemental oxygen.

209
00:13:52,080 --> 00:13:56,080
Now neurologic injury includes both stroke and traumatic brain injury.

210
00:13:56,080 --> 00:14:00,480
And the idea is that those reactive oxygen species that we've been talking about all

211
00:14:00,480 --> 00:14:07,000
along, they can cause further injury, worse outcomes, or secondary damage in either one

212
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:10,120
of these cases, either stroke or traumatic brain injury.

213
00:14:10,120 --> 00:14:13,800
Now that said, it does get a little bit more complicated than that.

214
00:14:13,800 --> 00:14:18,880
So we know that hypoxia results in worse outcomes for patients.

215
00:14:18,880 --> 00:14:24,880
So there's studies both for stroke and for TBI that demonstrate this fairly clearly.

216
00:14:24,880 --> 00:14:31,280
That said, there's also the idea that hyperoxia, so too much oxygen, can result in decreased

217
00:14:31,280 --> 00:14:32,440
cerebral blood flow.

218
00:14:32,440 --> 00:14:36,240
Now, the mechanism is not super well understood.

219
00:14:36,240 --> 00:14:40,360
It could be related to those reactive oxygen species that we just showed.

220
00:14:40,360 --> 00:14:47,960
It also could be correlated with vasoconstriction and vasodilation related to arterial CO2.

221
00:14:47,960 --> 00:14:50,600
So that's what these images here show.

222
00:14:50,600 --> 00:14:58,060
So basically as your CO2 increases, then your cerebral blood flow also increases.

223
00:14:58,060 --> 00:15:00,280
And then you have an inverse relationship with oxygen.

224
00:15:00,280 --> 00:15:07,240
As your O2 increases in your arterial blood, then you get a decrease in cerebral blood

225
00:15:07,240 --> 00:15:09,760
flow because your body thinks that it already has enough.

226
00:15:09,760 --> 00:15:11,880
So that's another proposed mechanism.

227
00:15:11,880 --> 00:15:17,880
So again, not super clear there, but by either one of these ways, it is known that hyperoxia

228
00:15:18,440 --> 00:15:21,920
or too much oxygen can decrease cerebral blood flow.

229
00:15:21,920 --> 00:15:28,040
So moving on with that idea, hyperoxia is when we have too much oxygen in the bloodstream.

230
00:15:28,040 --> 00:15:31,540
So with stroke, we do see decrease in blood flow.

231
00:15:31,540 --> 00:15:33,060
So that has been demonstrated.

232
00:15:33,060 --> 00:15:37,440
That said, we also do see some neuroprotection.

233
00:15:37,440 --> 00:15:39,800
And the mechanism here is by reducing inflammation.

234
00:15:39,800 --> 00:15:44,280
Now, we have a similar sort of conundrum with traumatic brain injury.

235
00:15:44,280 --> 00:15:48,400
We already talked about how the reactive oxygen species can cause damage, but we also

236
00:15:48,400 --> 00:15:55,000
know that giving oxygen can improve outcomes if it's done early after a TBI.

237
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:58,640
And there's another study more recently that shows that it doesn't actually worsen outcomes.

238
00:15:58,640 --> 00:16:01,760
So we have a little bit of a complicated picture on this one.

239
00:16:01,760 --> 00:16:06,880
And you'll see that in just a couple of slides, we show a summary chart that kind of outlines

240
00:16:06,880 --> 00:16:09,000
when we should and shouldn't give oxygen.

241
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:14,880
And the general consensus these days is that in stroke, you withhold oxygen unless somebody

242
00:16:14,880 --> 00:16:17,040
is hypoxic.

243
00:16:17,040 --> 00:16:21,760
In TBI, it's a little bit more acceptable to give oxygen.

244
00:16:21,760 --> 00:16:28,280
That said, this neuroprotection with administration of supplements oxygen for stroke patients,

245
00:16:28,280 --> 00:16:32,560
it has been demonstrated, but it hasn't really changed, practiced or entered into how we

246
00:16:32,560 --> 00:16:34,060
do things yet.

247
00:16:34,180 --> 00:16:40,420
Another paper to mention is this one from the Journal of Cerebral Blood Flow and Metabolism.

248
00:16:40,420 --> 00:16:45,460
So arguably the people who ought to know the most about this subject in general.

249
00:16:45,460 --> 00:16:51,660
So basically, they wanted to investigate this idea of cerebral blood flow and oxygen utilization

250
00:16:51,660 --> 00:16:54,140
in the brain based on FiO2.

251
00:16:54,140 --> 00:17:01,060
They used a novel technique to measure oxygen metabolism in the brain.

252
00:17:01,060 --> 00:17:08,100
So basically they found that in the hyperoxic states, the metabolism is decreased and in

253
00:17:08,100 --> 00:17:10,380
hypoxic states, it's increased.

254
00:17:10,380 --> 00:17:14,720
So same trend we sometimes see with measures of cerebral blood flow.

255
00:17:14,720 --> 00:17:18,100
In a hypoxic state, we increase it.

256
00:17:18,100 --> 00:17:23,580
And in a hyperoxic state, or when we give supplemental oxygen, we actually see a decrease

257
00:17:23,580 --> 00:17:25,300
in cerebral blood flow.

258
00:17:25,300 --> 00:17:29,540
Let's summarize here regarding worse outcomes in neurologic injury.

259
00:17:29,540 --> 00:17:36,420
Yes, hyperoxia can worsen outcomes, but also yes, hypoxia can worsen outcomes.

260
00:17:36,420 --> 00:17:42,100
Hyperoxia, so too much oxygen, can be neuroprotective, but it can also cause damage.

261
00:17:42,100 --> 00:17:46,260
So the moral here is that there are many, many factors at play, both with stroke and

262
00:17:46,260 --> 00:17:48,060
in traumatic brain injury.

263
00:17:48,060 --> 00:17:53,460
But just like with all the other points, the moral here is that use oxygen in moderation.

264
00:17:53,460 --> 00:17:55,220
All right, quick recap.

265
00:17:55,220 --> 00:18:00,740
These are the five potential adverse effects of oxygen that we already discussed.

266
00:18:00,740 --> 00:18:04,460
Now let's move on to some of the specifics.

267
00:18:04,460 --> 00:18:08,360
So first is indications, like when should we use oxygen?

268
00:18:08,360 --> 00:18:11,460
This chart here is just taken from a paper.

269
00:18:11,460 --> 00:18:13,420
Feel free to follow the link to check that out.

270
00:18:13,420 --> 00:18:18,260
But we've just drawn arrows pointing to the things that are specific to us in the EMS

271
00:18:18,260 --> 00:18:19,260
setting.

272
00:18:19,260 --> 00:18:22,860
So we won't go through all of these points, but just know that the things in green are

273
00:18:22,860 --> 00:18:28,500
ones where it's totally fine to give oxygen per this chart, things in red, contraindicated,

274
00:18:28,500 --> 00:18:31,940
and then there's some nuance with the yellow ones.

275
00:18:31,940 --> 00:18:36,220
Again, feel free to pause the video, zoom in, take a look at some of the stuff we've

276
00:18:36,220 --> 00:18:39,980
already discussed and some of it we'll get to here in just a second.

277
00:18:39,980 --> 00:18:40,980
So that's indications.

278
00:18:40,980 --> 00:18:43,740
There are also some contraindications.

279
00:18:43,740 --> 00:18:47,340
This list here is from a website called deranged physiology.

280
00:18:47,340 --> 00:18:49,220
I think we cited it once before.

281
00:18:49,220 --> 00:18:51,400
It's one of our favorite places on the internet.

282
00:18:51,400 --> 00:18:57,040
But this basically is a list of when we might want to hold off on giving oxygen.

283
00:18:57,040 --> 00:19:00,800
And we've made a couple of annotations here based on, again, things we've already discussed

284
00:19:00,800 --> 00:19:03,640
and things that we'll discuss in just a second.

285
00:19:03,640 --> 00:19:04,640
All right.

286
00:19:04,640 --> 00:19:09,800
So situations in which giving 100% or pure oxygen is okay.

287
00:19:09,800 --> 00:19:13,600
So first is if we have a true bona fide hypoxia.

288
00:19:13,600 --> 00:19:15,480
So find to give oxygen.

289
00:19:15,480 --> 00:19:18,120
Carbon monoxide exposure, also fine.

290
00:19:18,120 --> 00:19:20,200
Dramatic brain injury, we already talked about.

291
00:19:20,200 --> 00:19:23,360
Or it depends, but in general, could be okay.

292
00:19:23,360 --> 00:19:26,000
Prolonged CPR, also fine.

293
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:32,280
Pre-oxygenation, for example, in anticipation of a procedure or RSI or intubation, totally

294
00:19:32,280 --> 00:19:33,280
fine.

295
00:19:33,280 --> 00:19:35,800
The next two require just a little bit of explanation.

296
00:19:35,800 --> 00:19:38,360
So the first one is decompression illness.

297
00:19:38,360 --> 00:19:42,120
So with these patients, we do want to give oxygen, but just know that we're not just

298
00:19:42,120 --> 00:19:45,960
giving oxygen to ensure oxygen delivery to the tissues.

299
00:19:45,960 --> 00:19:47,520
There's other reasons.

300
00:19:47,520 --> 00:19:51,400
And you can read more about that in the article cited here.

301
00:19:51,400 --> 00:19:55,320
The next one is something that I came across while I was putting this video together.

302
00:19:55,320 --> 00:20:00,040
And this is denitrogenation of air-filled cavities.

303
00:20:00,040 --> 00:20:01,720
So that sounds a little complicated.

304
00:20:01,720 --> 00:20:06,520
But basically, if you have a situation where there's like a pocket of air in the body that's

305
00:20:06,520 --> 00:20:14,120
causing problems, so for example, a pneumothorax, if you fill that cavity with oxygen, so instead

306
00:20:14,120 --> 00:20:18,880
of just regular air, which is mostly nitrogen, but if you slowly get that cavity all the

307
00:20:18,880 --> 00:20:24,080
way full of oxygen, that oxygen will then make its way into the bloodstream and it could

308
00:20:24,080 --> 00:20:27,600
potentially reduce the size of that cavity.

309
00:20:27,600 --> 00:20:33,160
And then a few cases here to point out where we often here discuss that we should give

310
00:20:33,160 --> 00:20:39,920
oxygen, but in reality, the risk of damage from reactive oxygen species may actually

311
00:20:39,920 --> 00:20:41,760
outweigh the benefit.

312
00:20:41,760 --> 00:20:45,240
So we won't talk about these in depth, but there are articles linked for each one of

313
00:20:45,240 --> 00:20:46,240
them.

314
00:20:46,240 --> 00:20:51,240
The three of these are altitude illness, intrauterine resuscitation, and sepsis.

315
00:20:51,240 --> 00:20:57,000
So in all of these cases, it is often taught to give 100% oxygen, but that may actually

316
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:58,240
cause more damage.

317
00:20:58,240 --> 00:21:04,240
Okay, next thing to mention is that sometimes it is fine to give oxygen in high flow up

318
00:21:04,240 --> 00:21:07,520
to an SPO2 of 100% and maintain that.

319
00:21:07,520 --> 00:21:13,440
So the two reasons we would want to do that would be carbon monoxide exposure or decompression

320
00:21:13,440 --> 00:21:18,200
illness, and then there's potentially a use case for those airfield problems that we just

321
00:21:18,200 --> 00:21:19,200
discussed.

322
00:21:19,200 --> 00:21:23,600
Let's just summarize here on 100% oxygen.

323
00:21:23,600 --> 00:21:29,440
So 100% oxygen delivery is okay until our SPO2 gets into a therapeutic range.

324
00:21:29,440 --> 00:21:31,520
So for example, over 90%.

325
00:21:31,520 --> 00:21:37,480
That said, continuing to give oxygen to maintain an SPO2 of 100% generally is not needed in

326
00:21:37,480 --> 00:21:40,200
except in a few specific use cases.

327
00:21:40,200 --> 00:21:44,920
Okay, one final point to make and then we'll wrap everything up.

328
00:21:44,920 --> 00:21:49,160
We want to know at what dose of oxygen are we even concerned about producing reactive

329
00:21:49,160 --> 00:21:50,720
oxygen species?

330
00:21:50,720 --> 00:21:55,640
We already mentioned that these are the problematic things in this whole process, but how do we

331
00:21:55,640 --> 00:21:58,160
know what is a safe dose of oxygen?

332
00:21:58,160 --> 00:22:02,840
The answer to that question is a little bit elusive, and the reason for that is because

333
00:22:02,840 --> 00:22:06,840
it's a function of intracellular partial pressure of oxygen.

334
00:22:06,840 --> 00:22:11,960
So you can see on this chart that at the low end, so when we're hypoxic intracellularly,

335
00:22:11,960 --> 00:22:16,720
we have a spike in the production of reactive oxygen species, and then at the high end,

336
00:22:16,720 --> 00:22:22,280
when we're hyperoxic inside the cells, we see an exponential increase in the production

337
00:22:22,280 --> 00:22:24,760
of these reactive oxygen species.

338
00:22:24,760 --> 00:22:31,440
But those numbers, those intracellular PO2 numbers don't correlate cleanly with an arterial

339
00:22:31,440 --> 00:22:33,960
partial pressure of oxygen.

340
00:22:33,960 --> 00:22:39,080
That said, based on the data we had that looks at complications from the use of supplemental

341
00:22:39,080 --> 00:22:44,880
oxygen, we can kind of assume that if we have a PO2 of 100 millimeters of mercury or less,

342
00:22:44,880 --> 00:22:46,200
we're probably fine.

343
00:22:46,200 --> 00:22:51,000
And then once we get up to 300 or so, that's when we start to see a lot of issues.

344
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:56,200
That's when we see an increase in damage caused by these reactive oxygen species.

345
00:22:56,200 --> 00:23:01,520
So to put some numbers to that, if we have a patient on, let's say, two liters per minute

346
00:23:01,520 --> 00:23:08,960
of oxygen via nasal cannula or an FI2 of about 30%, this correlates with a PAO2 of

347
00:23:08,960 --> 00:23:12,240
around 100, and that's probably fine in most cases.

348
00:23:12,240 --> 00:23:19,240
On the extreme end, once we get to a PAO2 of 300 or more, we definitely have damage.

349
00:23:19,240 --> 00:23:23,680
We definitely have an increase in the production of reactive oxygen species.

350
00:23:23,680 --> 00:23:29,000
That would correlate with an FI2 of about 60 or maybe giving oxygen via a non-rebreather

351
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:30,160
mask.

352
00:23:30,160 --> 00:23:35,560
So when we're using oxygen at that dose, we ought to have some concern, especially

353
00:23:35,560 --> 00:23:41,120
if we're beyond the point where we fixed a low SPO2.

354
00:23:41,120 --> 00:23:42,580
And then there's also a time component.

355
00:23:42,580 --> 00:23:47,000
So we also want to know how long does it take when we're giving supplemental oxygen for

356
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:50,480
the reactive oxygen species to be produced and then to cause damage.

357
00:23:50,480 --> 00:23:54,920
Again, there's not a really straightforward answer here, but when we're giving 100% or

358
00:23:54,920 --> 00:24:02,160
high flow oxygen, normally within five to 20 minutes, our PAO2, our arterial partial

359
00:24:02,160 --> 00:24:07,000
pressure of oxygen plateaus based on whatever FIO2 we're delivering.

360
00:24:07,000 --> 00:24:12,760
And then we can assume that in that same timeframe, our intracellular oxygen concentration also

361
00:24:12,760 --> 00:24:14,460
reaches the plateau.

362
00:24:14,460 --> 00:24:17,000
With low flow delivery, the timeline is the same.

363
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:21,720
Just recognize that we may not ever reach an intracellular oxygen concentration that

364
00:24:21,720 --> 00:24:27,200
results in the production of reactive oxygen species.

365
00:24:27,200 --> 00:24:30,760
Okay, let's end with just a quick review.

366
00:24:30,760 --> 00:24:32,080
So this is what we started with.

367
00:24:32,080 --> 00:24:34,600
These are the questions that we wanted to answer.

368
00:24:34,600 --> 00:24:37,880
So let's just put them all together with answers on one slide.

369
00:24:37,880 --> 00:24:40,480
So when should we utilize oxygen?

370
00:24:40,480 --> 00:24:46,960
In general, when our SPO2 is below goal or we have some other evidence of hypoxia.

371
00:24:46,960 --> 00:24:49,320
And then what about 100% oxygen?

372
00:24:49,320 --> 00:24:55,840
So we talked about these cases would be hypoxia, carbon monoxide, traumatic brain injury, CPR,

373
00:24:55,840 --> 00:25:00,380
peroxogenation, decompression illness, or airfield issues.

374
00:25:00,380 --> 00:25:04,520
And then what about when do we want an SPO2 of 100%?

375
00:25:04,520 --> 00:25:09,280
So again, those are carbon monoxide exposure, decompression illness, and then maybe those

376
00:25:09,280 --> 00:25:11,420
airfield issues.

377
00:25:11,420 --> 00:25:13,680
And then when should we withhold oxygen?

378
00:25:13,680 --> 00:25:19,480
So in general, once SPO2 is in range, we should start to titrate down.

379
00:25:19,480 --> 00:25:24,640
So oftentimes this is somewhere between 90 and 93, depending on what agency you work

380
00:25:24,640 --> 00:25:25,640
for.

381
00:25:25,640 --> 00:25:30,120
All right, so is oxygen the bad guy?

382
00:25:30,120 --> 00:25:35,600
Probably not at low flows, and then it depends at high flow.

383
00:25:35,600 --> 00:25:36,600
That's it.

384
00:25:36,600 --> 00:25:37,600
That's all we got.
